Having been to Rome 20 years ago, I was absolutely not prepared for the crowds that are there now. If you really want to visit these sites, be prepared to shuffle along in the midst of a crowd, all the way through. We had already been on the tour for 3 hours when we reached the Basilica, and had to leave at that point and miss the last half hour, as the attention of 3 teenage boys had absolutely gone by then. That said, people who were not on a tour did not have the skip-the-line admission that we did, so they were queuing for over an hours for entrance to the Sistine Chapel or the Basilica.

so pros
- well-organised tour, good at managing large numbers efficiently;
- well-informed tour guide that steers you towards the key things to look out for;
- you skip the line for admission at each place
- every stop on this tour is so rammed with people it is good to be part of a larger group, as that keeps you moving and stops you being swamped by everyone else;

And cons
- not the fault of the tour company, but these sites let in far too many people at once, making it feel like an amusement park rather than a site of great historical, artistic and religious significance;
- really hard to view the Sistine Chapel or the Basilica with the reverence they probably deserve, when they are packed to the brim with tourists taking selfies;
- so many tour guides in there that sometimes you cannot hear your own tour guide above another one, despite this tour having a headset;
- beggars outside the ticket office where you pick up the tour, were aggressive and swearing at people who did not give them money!!
